<h2>A chargesheet that sat in a drawer for four years</h2>
<p>The facts were stark. The FIR was registered on 9 January 2015 for offences under the Delhi Excise Act, 2009 — a statute whose relevant offences carry a maximum sentence of three years. According to the status report filed by the State itself, the chargesheet was prepared by a head constable and forwarded by the Assistant Commissioner of Police on 4 May 2018. It was then submitted to the trial court only on 3/4 November 2022. The investigation thus took over three years; the completed chargesheet then took over four more years to travel from the police file to the courtroom. The trial court had taken cognizance, rejecting a limitation objection, which brought the petitioner to the High Court.</p>

<h2>The constitutional frame</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Accusation</strong><p>The right to a speedy trial under Article 21 attaches from the point of accusation — not from the framing of charge.</p></div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Investigation</strong><p>Timely completion of investigation is itself part of that right; an investigation cannot drift indefinitely before culminating in a chargesheet.</p></div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Trial and verdict</strong><p>The guarantee runs through every stage up to the final verdict, because delay at any stage can impair the defence.</p></div>
</div>
<p>Applying that frame, the Court found that the delay was inordinate, that it was not attributable to any factor beyond the control of the investigating authorities, and that it had violated the petitioner's rights under Article 21. The proceedings arising from the FIR were quashed.</p>

<h2>Why departmental action is no answer</h2>
<p>The State pointed out that departmental action had been initiated against the head constable responsible. The Court's response is the most practically significant part of the order: internal discipline may address the officer's misconduct, but it neither explains the delay in a legally relevant sense nor repairs the injury to the accused. A prosecution does not become constitutionally tolerable because someone within the department was punished for making it late. For an accused, this means the existence of internal accountability proceedings is not a factor that weighs against quashing — if anything, it is an admission that the delay was unjustified.</p>

<h2>Using the order in practice</h2>
<p>Petitions of this kind are filed under the inherent jurisdiction of the High Court — formerly Section 482 of the CrPC, now Section 528 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023. Three practical lessons follow from this decision. First, obtain the status report and reconstruct the internal chronology: the date the chargesheet was prepared, the date it was forwarded, and the date it was filed are often three different dates, and the gaps between them are the case. Second, the gravity of the offence matters in both directions — in a minor excise matter carrying a three-year maximum, an eight-year pre-cognizance delay is almost impossible for the State to defend. Third, keep the limitation argument in reserve: where the offence carries a maximum of three years, the three-year limitation for taking cognizance (formerly Section 468 CrPC, now Section 514 BNSS) supplies an independent line of attack, although in this case the Court rested its decision on Article 21 rather than limitation.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>This article summarises a judicial order for general information and is not legal advice. Whether delay in a particular investigation justifies quashing depends on the length of the delay, its causes, the gravity of the offence and the prejudice caused — each case turns on its own record.</p>
</div>
